en

# Cross-Layer Attention Probing for Fine-Grained Hallucination Detection

MS

Malavika SureshRahaf Aljundi

IN

Ikechukwu Nkisi-Orji

NW

Nirmalie Wiratunga

## Abstract

With the large-scale adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) in various applications, there is a growing reliability concern due to their tendency to generate inaccurate text, i.e. hallucinations. In this work, we propose Cross-Layer Attention Probing (CLAP), a novel activation probing technique for hallucination detection, which processes the LLM activations across the entire residual stream as a joint sequence. Our empirical evaluations using five LLMs and three tasks show that CLAP improves hallucination detection compared to baselines on both greedy decoded responses as well as responses sampled at higher temperatures, thus enabling fine-grained detection, i.e. the ability to disambiguate hallucinations and non-hallucinations among different sampled responses to a given prompt. This allows us to propose a detect-then-mitigate strategy using CLAP to reduce hallucinations and improve LLM reliability compared to direct mitigation approaches. 
An alternative approach is "open-box" detection, which examines the internal activations of the model—the mathematical representations of information as it passes through the model's layers. Most existing open-box methods focus on specific parts of the model, such as the final layer or individual attention heads. Research suggests, however, that different layers of an LLM specialize in different types of information. For instance, early layers might focus on syntax, while middle and later layers handle factual recall and semantic reasoning.

The research introduces Cross-Layer Attention Probing (CLAP), a method that analyzes the entire sequence of internal activations across all layers of an LLM. By treating the "residual stream" (the information flowing through the layers) as a joint sequence, CLAP aims to identify the subtle patterns that distinguish a factual response from a hallucination.

Image: CLAP Architecture Figure 1: The architecture of CLAP involves collecting activations from every layer of the LLM, projecting them to a lower dimension, and processing them through a transformer encoder to predict if a response is a hallucination.

